{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לכן", :translit "lakhen", :gloss "therefore"} {:he "לכן אמור לבני ישראל", :translit "lakhen emor livnei yisra'el", :gloss "therefore say to the children of Israel"} {:he "גן נעול אחותי כלה", :translit "gan na'ul akhoti kalah", :gloss "a locked garden is My sister, My bride"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["לכן" "לכן אמור לבני ישראל"]}], :frame {:text "The verse לכן אמור opens the parashah in Leviticus 17:12, and the word לכן also opens a parashah here in Numbers 25:12. The phrase גן נעול אחותי כלה appears in Song of Songs 4:12, read allegorically as Israel kept secluded from immorality. Now approaching the land, Israel is found breached in forbidden relations.", :sources ["Leviticus 17:12" "Song of Songs 4:12"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לכן", :translit "lakhen", :gloss "therefore"} {:he "מדה במדה", :translit "midah ba-midah", :gloss "measure for measure"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "לכן", :b "מדה במדה"}], :frame {:text "The principle of measure-for-measure punishment appears in Sotah 8b–11a, where various biblical punishments are shown to correspond exactly to the sins that triggered them.", :sources ["Sotah 8b"]}} {:quotes [{:he "את בריתי שלום", :translit "et briti shalom", :gloss "My covenant of peace"} {:he "שלם", :translit "shalem", :gloss "whole / complete (written defectively)"} {:he "וי\"ו קטיעא", :translit "vav kati'a", :gloss "severed vav"} {:he "שלם", :translit "shalem", :gloss "whole"} {:he "מתנה שלמה אני נותן לו", :translit "matanah sheleimah ani noten lo", :gloss "a complete gift I give him"} {:he "יו\"ד", :translit "yud", :gloss "yod (ten)"} {:he "יו\"ד במילוי גי' כ'", :translit "yud be-milui gematria kaf", :gloss "yod spelled out in gematria equals kaf"} {:he "פנחס הוא אליהו", :translit "pinchas hu eliyahu", :gloss "Pinchas is Elijah"} {:he "אליה", :translit "eliyah", :gloss "Elijah (written defectively, without vav)"} {:he "יעקוב", :translit "ya'akuv", :gloss "Jacob (written fully, with vav)"} {:he "ו' מאליהו", :translit "vav me-eliyahu", :gloss "vav from Elijah"} {:he "יגל יעקב ישמח ישראל ישמח", :translit "yagel ya'akov yismah yisra'el yismah", :gloss "Jacob will rejoice, Israel will be glad"} {:he "משיח", :translit "mashiah", :gloss "messiah / anointed one"} {:he "אליהו שלם", :translit "eliyahu shalem", :gloss "Elijah whole"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Word שלום written defectively (שלם) — the vav is the vav kati'a discussed in Kiddushin"} {:kind :gematria, :a "יו\"ד במילוי", :b "כ'"} {:kind :other, :note "Pinchas is identified as Elijah; Elijah written defective (אליה), Jacob written plene (יעקוב) — the missing vav of Elijah taken by Jacob as pledge until messianic redemption"} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "ישמח", :spells "משיח"}], :frame {:text "Pinchas is identified with Elijah in the Talmud and midrash. The word שלום is written defectively as שלם in Numbers 25:12, and the Talmud in Kiddushin 66b discusses a vav kati'a in the word שלום. The name אליהו is written defectively as אליה in Kings II 1:3–8, while יעקוב is written plene with an extra vav in Leviticus 26:42. The missing vav from Elijah's name was taken by Jacob as a pledge until Elijah returns as herald of the Messiah (Leviticus Rabbah 30:5). The verse יגל יעקב ישמח ישראל ישמח is Isaiah 9:1, and the word ישמח shares the letters of משיח.", :sources ["Kiddushin 66b" "Leviticus Rabbah 30:5" "Kings II 1:3" "Leviticus 26:42" "Isaiah 9:1"]}} {:quotes [{:he "שלום", :translit "shalom", :gloss "peace"} {:he "משיח", :translit "mashiah", :gloss "messiah / anointed one"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "שלום", :b "משיח"}], :frame {:text "The covenant of peace granted to Pinchas carries a messianic resonance, as the gematria of שלום equals that of משיח, linking the priestly covenant of peace to the anticipated messianic redemption.", :sources ["Numbers 25:12"]}} {:quotes [{:he "שלומיאל", :translit "shelumiel", :gloss "Shelumiel"} {:he "זמרי בן סלוא", :translit "zimri ben salu'a", :gloss "Zimri son of Salu"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "שלומיאל", :b "זמרי בן סלוא"}], :frame {:text "Shelumiel son of Zurishaddai is the prince of the tribe of Simeon listed in Numbers 1:6. Zimri son of Salu, identified as a leader of a Simeonite ancestral house in Numbers 25:14, was the Israelite slain by Pinchas. The equal gematria links the names of two prominent Simeonite figures.", :sources ["Numbers 1:6" "Numbers 25:14"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["לכן" "לכן אמור לבני ישראל"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "לכן", :refs ["Genesis 4:15" "Genesis 30:15" "Exodus 6:6" "Numbers 16:11" "Numbers 20:12" "Numbers 25:12" "Judges 8:7" "Judges 10:13"], :n 192, :lens :exact} {:phrase "לכן אמור לבני ישראל", :refs ["Exodus 6:6"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :gematria, :a "לכן", :a-value 100, :b "מדה במדה", :b-value 100, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :other, :note "Word שלום written defectively (שלם) — the vav is the vav kati'a discussed in Kiddushin", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "יו\"ד במילוי", :a-value 118, :b "כ'", :b-value 20, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :other, :note "Pinchas is identified as Elijah; Elijah written defective (אליה), Jacob written plene (יעקוב) — the missing vav of Elijah taken by Jacob as pledge until messianic redemption", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "ישמח", :spells "משיח", :letters ["י"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch} {:kind :gematria, :a "שלום", :a-value 376, :b "משיח", :b-value 358, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "שלומיאל", :a-value 417, :b "זמרי בן סלוא", :b-value 406, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:31:47.166069774Z", :model "glm-5.2"}